extends "user://mod/core/TASTool/12_action_log.gd"

func _show_changelog_if_needed() -> void:
	var seen_version := str(SavedSettings.get_value(SETTING_CHANGELOG_VERSION, ""))
	if seen_version == GOOBPLAYABILITY_VERSION:
		return
	SavedSettings.set_value(SETTING_CHANGELOG_VERSION, GOOBPLAYABILITY_VERSION)
	var message := "Version %s\n\n" % GOOBPLAYABILITY_VERSION
	message += "• Autoplay no longer treats the chosen duration as the end of a long level. It begins with a 30-second route budget and automatically extends verified paths up to three minutes, retaining the exact frontier state at each extension. Stitched search branches now carry their moving-platform phase into Autoplay and Macro Bot playback, and a timing-only replay death is recovered from the next verified frame. Partial paths are labelled as learned prefixes until the finish has actually been reached.\n\n"
	message += "• Autoplay now maps large, unusual levels without cutting off their geometry: its route budget covers the full 2,337-object Hashira test level and the mapper uses fast spatial merging plus a priority heap. If hundreds of animated pieces prevent a complete static finish route, it uses a reachable checkpoint as an intermediate landmark, replans immediately from every real checkpoint contact, and retains the best connected route prefix instead of falling back to blind spawn movement. The currently tested level is also saved to TAS diagnostics so any remaining mapper mismatch can be inspected exactly.\n\n"
	message += "• Autoplay now keeps useful mid-jump, wall-launch, and apex states instead of discarding every route that has not landed yet. Wall climbing reacts to real wall contacts: it jumps away, returns to the face at several tested timings, and jumps again from the higher contact; a separate alternating-wall pattern handles shafts. Static levels also stop treating identical wall positions at different clock times as new progress, and the frontier explicitly explores all four map extremes so unusual routes are not starved.\n\n"
	message += "• Window resizing now keeps the chosen top-left position.\n"
	message += "• Timeline freecam keeps the native SDF/themed level presentation active and includes Center and Reset controls.\n"
	message += "• Cosmetic catalogs receive a bounded readiness retry.\n"
	message += "• Replay Self-Test rejects incomplete diagnostic coverage and identifies the first failing system.\n"
	message += "• Moving platforms and animations are included in Debug Mode determinism checks.\n"
	message += "• Optional verified updates and one-click rollback are available in Goober Dash Settings.\n"
	message += "• Fixed a resize deadlock in the shared window geometry helper that could jump the Timeline or Sandbox panel to a tiny size and lock it there.\n"
	message += "• Fixed levels rendering as hollow shapes with a colored outline and no terrain fill -- this happens when a block/ramp is too thin for the level's theme to round its corners and draw its stroke layers. Goobplayability now always renders every block/ramp's own shape underneath the normal terrain, so nothing is ever left hollow (a tiny square corner may occasionally peek out past a rounded terrain corner as a side effect).\n"
	message += "• Fixed physics blocks and ice blocks still going invisible in the Timeline Editor after jump zones and sawblades were already fixed by the previous update -- physics blocks had their own script actively fighting the fix every frame, which is now turned off during Timeline Editor preview, and ice blocks get an extra targeted safety net on top of the existing fix.\n\n"
	message += "• Extended the invisible-object fix to every remaining object type in the game (backgrounds, cannons, checkpoints, disappearing blocks, gravity fields, lasers, music blocks, pits, rechargers, signs, the start/finish line, and more) instead of only the specific ones reported so far -- scoped strictly to the Timeline Editor preview, so anything that's meant to disappear or change during actual gameplay (like disappearing blocks) still behaves normally when actually playing.\n\n"
	message += "• Added an experimental \"Claude Experimental Mode\" toggle (Settings -> Client Tools) that swaps in icon art next to menu titles/tabs. Off by default -- nothing changes unless you turn it on.\n\n"
	message += "• Claude Experimental Mode now also applies a full flat \"modern black\" reskin to every Goobplayability window (main panel, Action Log, Cosmetic Sandbox, Timeline Editor, Cheat Menu) -- near-black panels, thin hairline borders, and no text outline/shadow, instead of the classic navy pill look. Every existing function, button, and layout works exactly as before; only colors and surface styling changed. Still fully opt-in and off by default.\n\n"
	message += "• Follow-up polish for Claude Experimental Mode: title icons no longer show their baked-in dark tile background (it's keyed out to transparent so only the glyph shows, blending into the panel), and buttons across every window are flatter and more muted (no ring border, darker accent tones) instead of looking like bright candy-colored pills on a black background.\n\n"
	message += "• Claude Experimental Mode now also swaps the game's rounded Baloo font for a clean sans-serif font (Inter) across every menu, matching a modern dashboard look instead of reading as reused game-HUD text. If the font file isn't installed, everything quietly falls back to Baloo exactly as before.\n\n"
	message += "• Claude Experimental Mode restructures the main Goobplayability panel: the pill tab strip is replaced by a flat nav row, and every control that used to sit in one long unlabeled list is now grouped into bordered, titled cards (Frame Rate, Visual Debug, Input Display, Playback Speed, Frame Stepping, Perfect Jumpzone, Macro Bot Mode, Checkpoints, Macro Playback, Macro Slots). Every button, checkbox, and function is unchanged -- only how they're organized on screen.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed Claude Experimental Mode's colors actually being a darkened copy of GooberDash's own blue/pink instead of a real color change -- and fixed several headings (the GOOBPLAYABILITY title, ACTION LOG, DEBUG TOOLS, and the new card headers) that were skipping the color theme entirely and staying classic blue/pink.\n\n"
	message += "• Replaced that first color attempt (five equally loud accent colors at once, which read as unprofessional/cluttered) with a restrained palette: most buttons are now a calm neutral dark gray, with one vivid teal reserved for whichever button/tab is actually active or primary, red only for genuinely destructive actions (Clear, delete), green for play, and violet for Edit Timeline. Titles render in plain white instead of colored text, matching a modern flat-dashboard look instead of a rainbow of GooberDash accent colors.\n\n"
	message += "• Added the Replay Hub: browse community replays (Featured / Newest / By Level) and download or one-click Play any of them -- they import into a brand new Macro Slot, so every existing Play/Edit/Delete control works on them exactly like a macro you recorded yourself. It's its own independent window, not part of the main Goobplayability panel -- enable it from Settings -> Client Tools -> COMMUNITY REPLAY HUB, same as the Avatar Sandbox GUI toggle, which then shows its own small access button.\n\n"
	message += "• The Replay Hub now supports uploading: pick a saved Macro Slot and share it with a title/author of your choice, or use COPY SQL instead to paste it in by hand. There's still no account system, so uploads are anonymous and can't be edited or removed from the client after the fact.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed the Replay Hub rejecting real Macro Slot uploads as \"too large\" -- real slots run around 8 MB and the old limit was 256 KB. Replay data is now gzip-compressed before upload/export whenever that helps, and every size limit (client and Supabase) was raised with real headroom above that.\n\n"
	message += "• The Replay Hub now follows Claude Experimental Mode too -- it was left classic-only when it was first added, which is why it looked out of place next to every other reskinned window. It has no approved title icon of its own yet, so only its colors/font change, not an icon.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed Claude Experimental Mode not actually reskinning the Replay Hub, Cosmetic Sandbox, or Timeline Editor despite the toggle being on -- each of those windows builds its whole UI immediately when it's created, but the on/off signal from Settings was arriving slightly too late to affect that, every single time, so they silently stayed classic-colored no matter what the saved setting said. They now read the setting for themselves at startup instead of waiting to be told.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed Timeline Editor panels (Timeline, Details, Freecam) getting stuck unable to resize bigger even with plenty of empty screen space -- a panel positioned away from the top-left corner only had a sliver of room to grow into on its own side of the screen. Dragging a resize grip now uses the whole screen as the limit, nudging the panel's position back just enough to make room when it would otherwise run off the edge, instead of refusing to grow further.\n\n"
	message += "• Added a search bar to Goober Dash's own Leaderboard tab (above the Global/Local list) -- type a username or a full user ID and it opens that player's normal profile dialog, exactly like clicking any leaderboard row. It resolves a username through the same Nakama client library the game already ships with; Winterpixel just never wired a UI to it. No new information is shown beyond what any leaderboard entry already displays, and it doesn't touch or add any online/presence status.\n\n"
	message += "• Added a 2-second cooldown to the Leaderboard tab's new player search: submitting a search (by button or Enter) disables it and blocks every path -- including the free direct-user-ID lookup -- until the cooldown clears, measured from the moment you search rather than from when the result comes back. There's no way to fire off searches back-to-back faster than that, whether by clicking fast, mixing Enter and the button, or a slow response coming back late.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed the Leaderboard tab's new player search causing the game to fail to load entirely -- one of the added lines referenced a Control-only constant from a script that doesn't extend Control, which is a hard error and took down the whole mod. Also added real error detail (HTTP status and message) to the Action Log when a username search fails, since \"Search failed\" alone wasn't useful for tracking down why.\n\n"
	message += "• The Leaderboard tab's player search no longer attempts username lookups -- confirmed against the real server that it always returns 401 Forbidden, so Winterpixel's server itself doesn't allow it for a regular player session (this isn't fixable client-side). The search field now only accepts a full user ID, which still works exactly like clicking a leaderboard row, and says clearly why a username won't work instead of trying and failing.\n\n"
	message += "• The Leaderboard tab's player search now has its own Settings -> Client Tools toggle (LEADERBOARD PLAYER SEARCH), on by default, matching every other client tool instead of always being on with no way to turn it off.\n\n"
	message += "• Added a \"HOST WITH CODE\" option to the Custom Lobby screen, next to CREATE LOBBY -- type your own code instead of getting a random one, and it hosts a new lobby under that code (or joins one that already exists under it, same as typing a friend's code into JOIN). This reuses join_or_create_preset_lobby(), a function the game already ships and already uses for Discord's own party-join flow -- no new or restricted server calls, just a button for a path that only existed for Discord before. Has its own Settings -> Client Tools toggle (CUSTOM LOBBY CODE), on by default.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed the Custom Lobby code button breaking the whole game again, same category of mistake as before: this time it was `var script_ref: = scene.get_script()` -- typing that variable with := makes it a Reference, and Reference has no get_script_constant_map() method, only the actual Script subclass does, so the call didn't resolve and the whole script failed to compile. Left it as a plain untyped var instead, matching how this exact call is already done safely elsewhere in this file.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed the Custom Lobby code box being impossible to click into or type in: the CREATE LOBBY panel isn't a layout container, so the input row was landing in the corner at zero width. It now sits in the panel's real container, directly under the \"Create a new private lobby\" text, with a proper minimum width.\n\n"
	message += "• Fixed HOST WITH CODE appearing to ignore the code you typed. The lobby actually was being registered under your code on the server -- but Goober Dash's own create_preset_lobby() names its parameter the same thing as the variable the on-screen code label reads from, so it updated the parameter and left the label blank. (Winterpixel never noticed because its only user is the Discord join button, which hides that label anyway.) Hosting now does the lobby-code write directly instead of going through that function, so the code on screen is the real one -- and if the server ever refuses a code, it now says so instead of failing silently.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Enabled the game's dormant Friends and Party systems through a new Friends & Party window. It repairs the unfinished friend-code display, exposes incoming/outgoing friend requests and presence, creates four-player squads, handles party invitations and members, and feeds the squad directly into Goober Dash's existing party-aware PLAY matchmaking path.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Added a Game Tools window (Settings -> Client Tools -> GAME TOOLS, off by default) with an Infinite Dash tool, in its own GameTools.gd script. It targets the current local player without excluding any match type. Online games remain server-authoritative, so server snapshots may overwrite client-side changes. Anything unexpected still fails closed, but Game Tools does not change replay or leaderboard eligibility.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Game Tools now writes the dash cooldown from both of Godot's per-frame callbacks instead of only the physics one, since Goober Dash steps its own tick loop and which callback lands after a given tick isn't something worth assuming. The window also now reports every relevant link in the chain live -- whether a game was found, its network type, whether your player was found, and the actual dash_cooldown and dash_timer values -- so when a tool has no effect it says which step is responsible instead of just sitting there.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Fixed Game Tools failing to compile, which took the whole Goobplayability GUI down with it. Two fixes: the bad line itself (a variable typed with := from an expression the compiler had no type for), and -- more importantly -- every optional module is now checked with can_instance() before being created. Previously one broken module aborted the rest of startup and you lost the entire tool; now a broken module is skipped on its own and everything else still loads.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Added six named Cosmetic Loadout slots to the Customize screen. Each slot saves the currently equipped colour, suit, hat, hand item and four emotes, then equips the whole set through Goober Dash's normal account service so it follows you into matches.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Added an Editor Theme Pack with Cosmic Dots, Neon Grid and Sunset Circuit. Cosmic Dots follows the requested starry reference, including dotted terrain and star-crate visual variants; all blocks retain their original Goober Dash physics and node types.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Fixed the Editor Theme Pack producing blank theme entries and repeated resource_name errors. Custom themes are now real BaseTheme resources and their buttons are inserted through the editor's native theme-button scene, so Cosmic Dots, Neon Grid and Sunset Circuit can actually be selected.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Refined the Editor Theme Pack and removed its public-match overhead. It now wakes only for editor levels and reacts to theme/load/place events instead of repeatedly scanning live matches. Platforms keep Goober Dash's stock shapes and outlines, with visual-only recolours and surface art; native saws, spikes, arrows, alerts and signs receive restrained theme treatment only where needed. Cosmic Dots now uses a sparse permanent parallax star field, while placeable background walls use separate spacecraft-panel artwork.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Fixed the refined Editor Theme Pack failing at runtime in this game's custom Godot build. Its image recolourer now uses a compatible validity check, custom theme buttons directly apply the selected BaseTheme to both halves of the playable editor, and the normally near-transparent tiling layer is raised only for custom themes so Cosmic's permanent stars are actually visible. Alert, arrow and sign colours are also strong enough to read clearly while retaining the stock art.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Reworked custom-theme platforms and physics crates to stay inside Goober Dash's native art style. Platforms now recolour the stock terrain motif while retaining the game's original corner radii, outline widths, one-way floors, checkpoints and finish-line textures. Physics crates now recolour the real stock crate image at its exact nine-slice dimensions, keeping its original star, rivets, borders and shading and eliminating the stretched horizontal artifacts from the generated replacement.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Refined the custom themes again so their stock artwork also fits the subject: Cosmic uses Goober Dash's own star terrain motif, Neon uses its city diagonal motif, and Sunset uses its dashed motif, all palette-mapped rather than redrawn. The native one-way platform, checkpoint and finish-line art is now recoloured too, eliminating leftover green/brown strips. On the Customize screen, Sandbox now occupies the top-left and Loadouts the top-right instead of sharing the same right-side area or covering the stock tabs.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Moved the Customize screen's Sandbox button out from under the top-left profile overlay, which made the visible button impossible to click. Loadouts and Sandbox now form a separated top-right column, with Sandbox directly below Loadouts and both clear of the native cosmetic tabs.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Added Public Match Map Preview. On joining a normal public game it reads the complete map set already supplied by the game server and shows the 32P/16P/8P/4P sequence, map type, author and rating. A MAPS button keeps it reopenable during the match.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Added a separate Highest Wins board inside Goober Dash's Leaderboard tab, powered by twhlynch's daily community stats. It saves validated UTC snapshots automatically, supports importing the site's dated wins CSV downloads, and offers All Time, Daily, Monthly and Yearly views. Period views only appear when their exact boundary snapshots exist, so missing history is shown honestly instead of estimated.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Added an Autoplay tab beside Macro Bot. It learns locally by running real physics/input attempts from the current position, retaining elite routes plus a spatial exploration archive and mutating their movement, jump and dash timing. Training can be stopped and continued, and the best attempt can be deterministically replayed or sent to Macro Bot as an editable segment. Autoplay explicitly supports Time Trial/editor tests/tutorials only; public matches remain server-authoritative and are rejected with a clear message.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Autoplay now applies useful knowledge immediately instead of restarting every mutation from an unprotected opening. Once an attempt gains enough distance or reaches a checkpoint, that successful prefix is locked and replayed with Macro Bot's authoritative state correction on every later attempt; only the new suffix is mutated. A failed ending is discarded while the learned beginning stays, so training advances through the level progressively.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Replaced Autoplay's geometric-route execution with real-physics frontier search. Geometry is now only a search hint: the bot repeatedly restores positions it genuinely reached, branches into varied running, short/held/delayed jumps, wall-jump pulses, reversals, coasting and differently timed dashes, then retains novel surviving endpoints including their real velocity, grounded/wall state, dash cooldown and moving-level time. Useful branches are assembled into the best playable path immediately instead of repeating one failed opening toward an assumed waypoint.\n\n"
	message += "\u2022 Fixed Autoplay misreading Goober Dash checkpoint IDs as an increasing score. It now remembers the actual checkpoint chain, retains intermediate landing/wall/checkpoint states, spreads its frontier across distinct map regions, and rotates between checkpoint, depth, breadth, exploration, and goal priorities. Airborne states also use dedicated steering, landing-jump, jump-zone, and dash branches instead of wasting most probes on impossible repeated ground jumps.\n\n"
	message += "The TAS client is now named Goobplayability. This changelog appears once for each released version."
	_show_responsive_popup("GOOBPLAYABILITY — WHAT'S NEW", message)
